WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.696
In many Indian homes, there is a small
Tulsi plant.

00:00:06.196 --> 00:00:11.716
Every morning, someone may offer water,
light a lamp or pause beside

00:00:11.716 --> 00:00:13.876
it for a moment of prayer.

00:00:14.376 --> 00:00:20.544
But during the sacred month of Kartik,
something extraordinary happens.

00:00:21.044 --> 00:00:24.932
The Tulsi plant is adorned like a bride.

00:00:25.432 --> 00:00:32.426
Fresh flowers surround it. Sugarcane
stalks form a wedding canopy. Lamps are

00:00:32.426 --> 00:00:38.512
lit, families gather, and traditional
wedding songs fill the home.

00:00:39.012 --> 00:00:43.524
But why is a sacred plant married to Lord
Vishnu?

00:00:44.024 --> 00:00:50.087
To understand this beautiful tradition, we
must travel into an ancient Puranic

00:00:50.087 --> 00:00:50.624
story.

00:00:51.124 --> 00:00:54.868
The story of Tulasi and Shankhachuda.

00:00:55.368 --> 00:01:01.263
Long ago, according to the sacred
narrative, there lived a powerful ruler

00:01:01.263 --> 00:01:02.856
named Shankhachuda.

00:01:03.356 --> 00:01:09.428
His wife, Tulasi, was renowned for her
devotion and steadfastness.

00:01:09.928 --> 00:01:16.375
Shankhachuda possessed divine armour that
protected him in battle. His protection

00:01:16.375 --> 00:01:21.328
was also connected to the integrity of his
marriage to Tulasi.

00:01:21.828 --> 00:01:27.156
Then came a great conflict between
Shankhachuda and the gods.

00:01:27.656 --> 00:01:30.992
Even Lord Shiva entered the battle.

00:01:31.492 --> 00:01:36.676
Yet Shankhachuda remained protected, and
the struggle continued.

00:01:37.176 --> 00:01:42.923
Lord Vishnu eventually approached him
disguised as an elderly Brahmin and

00:01:42.923 --> 00:01:45.408
obtained his protective armour.

00:01:45.908 --> 00:01:51.668
Then Vishnu assumed Shankhachuda's
appearance and came before Tulasi.

00:01:52.168 --> 00:01:56.896
Believing her husband had returned, Tulasi
received him.

00:01:57.396 --> 00:02:00.996
But the deception did not remain hidden.

00:02:01.496 --> 00:02:07.472
When Tulasi discovered the truth, she was
overcome with grief and anger.

00:02:07.972 --> 00:02:13.009
She condemned Vishnu for what he had done
and pronounced a curse:

00:02:13.009 --> 00:02:14.764
he would become stone.

00:02:15.264 --> 00:02:20.472
According to this Puranic account, Vishnu
accepted the curse.

00:02:20.972 --> 00:02:26.866
The story then describes his association
with the sacred Shaligram stone, while

00:02:26.866 --> 00:02:32.612
Tulasi becomes associated with the Gandaki
River and the revered Tulsi plant.

00:02:33.112 --> 00:02:39.032
And so, in this devotional tradition,
Tulsi and Shaligram came to hold

00:02:39.032 --> 00:02:41.200
a special place together.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:47.532
That ancient association is remembered
every year through Tulsi Vivah.

00:02:48.032 --> 00:02:53.989
The Tulsi plant is dressed and decorated
as a bride. Shaligram, or

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:59.768
another representation of Lord Vishnu, is
honoured as the groom.

00:03:00.268 --> 00:03:06.473
Families gather around the decorated
plant, perform customary wedding rituals,

00:03:06.473 --> 00:03:10.636
light lamps, sing auspicious songs and
share prasad.

00:03:11.136 --> 00:03:17.556
The customs may differ between regions and
households, but the celebration continues

00:03:17.556 --> 00:03:21.408
to connect devotion, nature and family
traditions.

00:03:21.908 --> 00:03:26.276
Wishing you and your family a blessed
Tulsi Vivah.

00:03:26.776 --> 00:03:32.683
This is an accessible literary retelling
based on the Devi Bhagavata Purana.

00:03:32.683 --> 00:03:38.590
Other Puranic and regional traditions
preserve different versions of Tulsi's

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:39.664
sacred story.
